course: dictionary
lesson: 4252
title: One word, many meanings: grass
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Grass is a word you know well, but it can mean more than one thing. Its main meaning is the green plants with thin leaves that cover lawns, parks and fields. «Keep off the grass» means you should not walk on those plants. Here the word means the whole green covering.
explain: A grass can also mean one kind of this plant. Wheat and bamboo are grasses too, even though they do not look like the lawn outside your window. «The steppe grass grew tall» is a clue: the author is talking about many grasses together, growing wild across a flat land.
explain: Grass can also be a slang word. To grass on someone is to tell on them, which is a UK way of saying someone reported you. You will hear this in stories and playground talk, so read the words around it. If the sentence is about getting someone into trouble, grass means to tell on them.
explain: Some phrases use grass in a clever way. «The grass is always greener on the other side» means other things seem better than they really are. The sentence does not mean real grass. Like all idioms, you understand it by thinking about what the whole sentence is saying.
fact: Grass is the green plants with thin leaves that cover lawns, parks and fields.
fact: A grass is one kind of this plant; wheat and bamboo are grasses too.
fact: «Keep off the grass» means do not walk on the lawn.
fact: To grass on someone means to tell on them, a UK slang use.
fact: «The grass is always greener on the other side» means other things seem better than they are.
fact: Grass makes food from sunlight, and cows and sheep eat grass.
fact: Some grass can be very tall, and the phrase «heights of grass» means how tall the plants grow.
exercise: «Keep off the grass,» the sign said. What is grass in this sentence? | A tall tree with a rough bark | A small insect that hops | A kind of wet, muddy soil | *The green plants with thin leaves that cover lawns and parks
exercise: Which of these is a grass? | Oak tree | Sunflower | Rose bush | *Wheat
exercise: «Ireland's frequent rain keeps the grass green all year.» Why is the grass green all year? | *Because it rains often there | Because the sun never sets | Because cows are kept off it | Because the grass has been painted
test: «Chornozem was built over thousands of years by steppe grass roots dying and rotting into the ground.» What built the black earth? | *Grass roots dying and rotting into the ground | Herds of wildebeest walking over it | Rivers of melted ice | Volcanoes flowing down the hills
test: «The grass is always greener on the other side» means: | People in other countries are always happy | Grass grows best on the far side of the fence | Other people's lawns are always mown | *Other things seem better than they really are
exam: «No Mow May» asks people to skip mowing for a month, so that clover and daisies can bloom. Why do the flowers matter? | *They feed bees and butterflies | They make the lawn turn brown in the sun | They stop the grass from growing at all | They tell the neighbours to keep off the grass
